WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.530
Hey everyone! What's happening?

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:02.820
I was thinking the other day

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.880
And I realized that while my day in the life video

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.900
is cool, and chill and all. It doesn't really do a great job of explaining

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:15.360
What exactly I do at my job, and I figured that's probably something you all want to know.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.520
So today I'm going to talk in detail about what I do at work.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.220
Okay, let's begin!

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.520
Throughout my career I've been mostly focused on writing mobile apps

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.520
and to be specific, when I mean mobile apps,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.300
I mean apps that tend to be more productivity or social focused.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.860
These are some of the apps that I worked on.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.060
I'm what some people call a product engineer since the engineering that I do

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.680
is directly related to the product that people use.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.680
Some engineers are not product engineers by contrast

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.360
and they work to provide services for other software engineers

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.200
or make improvements to websites and apps that they run faster

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.480
and so that they're up and running all the time.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.540
So in order to talk a little bit about my responsibilities as a product engineer,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.280
let's dive in and talk a little bit about the product process.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.460
We have a product manager or PM, who kicks off this whole process by deciding on what we're building.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.980
It then gets handed over to a designer who figures out how is this thing gonna look?

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.780
and then finally it gets handed over to the engineer who builds the thing,

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.320
so let's walk through a simple example

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:18.360
So say that you and I are working on an app for food delivery. The product manager might say

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:24.800
Hey, let's put a button on the home screen so that users can order their favorite dish from their favorite restaurant on one tap.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.700
The designer would then take it and then figure out how it's going to look on the screen by creating mock-ups

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.920
The mock-ups then get handed over to the engineer who implements and ships the feature to its users.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:41.260
The whole process is actually very collaborative and is usually not divided up like how I described as an assembly line.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.720
What actually happens is that we all provide our own expertise

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.700
but then we end up making a lot of decisions together.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.900
So I would say a solid 20 percent of my time is spent doing exactly this.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.920
I should also mention that this process moves super quickly.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.500
It's not uncommon for us to conceive an idea and get it out within a week or two.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.140
Because there are several people involved in this process

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:06.440
to keep everyone in the loop, we have frequent meetings in order to communicate what we're all working on

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:14.220
Ok! So 20% of my time is spent working with PM's, designers, and other engineers to discuss what we're working on

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:15.820
Next

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.460
70% of my time is spent building stuff.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.660
So based on the discussions we've had with the product manager and designer,

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.020
it's up to me and the other engineers to build the thing.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.020
My process usually goes something like this...

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:32.060
Number one: figure out a plan for how to build the thing. I asked myself questions like

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.960
do I need to coordinate with other teams in my company in order to accomplish this?

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.660
What classes am I gonna make?

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.980
Should I use what Apple's provided me, or should I make my own custom version of it?

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:45.520
How am I going to format the data that I want to show in the way that I want?

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.900
And once I have a plan, sometimes I'll review with other engineers to make sure that my plan makes sense.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.040
So lastly, I go and code according to my plan

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.920
and sometimes it goes according to plan and sometimes it doesn't

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:02.920
But it certainly helps to have a plan so that it saves time and frustration.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:06.960
In addition to building features, I will be fixing bugs in the mean time

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.240
or making improvements to our tests and tooling to make development easier.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:16.700
So this whole portion of reading, writing and fixing code takes quite a bit of time because of all the little problems

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:21.060
I'm solving along the way and so usually a large portion of my day is spent doing this.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.960
and so the last 10% of my day is spent doing miscellaneous things.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:26.680
Things like:

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.740
Having one-on-ones with my manager and other people to talk about career stuff

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.400
Participating in meetings in other parts of the company to find out what everyone is working on

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.100
and just working and participating in any other areas that I'm passionate about at work.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.700
Okay So if my math is correct that comes out to a hundred percent.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:48.200
20% collaborating with cross-functional roles, 70% building and coding

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.220
and 10% miscellaneous things around the company

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:53.100
and not every day looks the same on the job

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.040
There are some days where I have meetings all day and I code only for an hour or so

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.600
and there are other days where I have no meetings and I code for the whole day

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.220
The variety of my days helped me to maintain a good balance.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:07.260
Anyways, I hope that gives a little more clarity into what I do at work. Thank you so much for watching!

